#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175707 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca7dee-1a1b-5af6-8719-5d2377004be9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Displacement of a Particle Given Its Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "f2597771-4f5e-5a90-9c39-d8b902690340" :question/math-academy-id "e-568" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t) = 2t + 3t^2$. What is the total displacement of the particle between $t=1$ and $t=3$?" :question/worked-solution "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= ∫_{1}^{3}(2t + 3t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= (t^{2} + t^{3}) \\mid _{1}^{3} \\\\\n&= [3^{2} + 3^{3}] - [1^{2} + 1^{3}] \\\\\n&= [9 + 27] - [1 + 1] \\\\\n&= 36 - 2 \\\\\n&= 34\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the total displacement between $t=1$ and $t=3$ is $34$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175719 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be9ac99c-424e-5e90-b26f-c26e4da704d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "15fea8a5-1937-5f81-80f3-3bc88a51bec6" :question/math-academy-id "e-7128" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t) = 6t^2 +4t$. At time $t=2$ the particle is located at the position $x=-20$. What is the position of the particle at time $t=4$?" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=4$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in[2,4]$. Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(4) = x(2) + \\int_2^4 v(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(4) &= -20 + ∫_{2}^{4}(6t^{2} + 4t)dt \\\\\n&=-20 + [2t^{3} + 2t^{2}]_{2}^{4} \\\\\n&=-20 + ([2(4)^{3} + 2(4)^{2}] - [2(2)^{3} + 2(2)^{2}]) \\\\\n&=-20 + ([128 + 32] - [16 + 8]) \\\\\n&=-20 + (160 - 24) \\\\\n&=-20 + 136 \\\\\n&= 116\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=4$ is $x=116$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175720 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089120d4-b37b-5936-8ea4-b68848290606" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle Given a Velocity-Time Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "ac3a9d8a-a877-574d-86ef-be3ca3f5b61e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7129" :question/problem "The graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=2$ the particle is located at the position $x=-1$. What is the position of the particle at time $t=8$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3576/Source/Images/1e5634171531dd04f9da7173af00e15c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t = 8$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,8]$. Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(8) = x(2)+ \\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(2)=-1$, and we can compute the integral $\\displaystyle\\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm dt$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=2$ to $t=8$. Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm dt = 6\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(8) = -1+ 6 = 5\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=8$ is $x=5$."}]}
